<h2>The background</h2>
<p>The applicant sought anticipatory bail in FIR No. 138/2026 of PS Jahangir Puri, registered under Sections 20, 61 and 29 of the NDPS Act. Two features framed the hearing before Justice Girish Kathpalia. First, the applicant had already been declared a proclaimed offender on 5 November 2026 — a declaration that follows a court&rsquo;s satisfaction that the person is absconding to evade process. Second, on the date of hearing, his counsel did not appear at either call, and the Court proceeded on the record with the assistance of the Additional Public Prosecutor, who questioned the very maintainability of the application.</p>

<h2>Maintainability: the door stays open</h2>
<p>The Court declined to hold the application non-maintainable. A proclaimed offender may still apply for anticipatory bail. That holding matters, because proclamation orders are sometimes treated as an automatic disqualification; the Court&rsquo;s approach keeps the judicial discretion alive rather than converting the proclamation into a statutory bar.</p>

<h2>Merits: the bar rises sharply</h2>
<p>Keeping the door open is not the same as walking through it. The Court held that an applicant who has been proclaimed must show exceptional circumstances justifying pre-arrest protection, or demonstrate that no offence is prima facie made out. The logic is straightforward: anticipatory bail is a discretionary, equitable remedy, and a person who has evaded the court&rsquo;s own process starts with conduct that weighs against the exercise of that discretion. On the record before it — an NDPS FIR, a subsisting proclamation, a co-accused whose own bail plea had been withdrawn, and no appearance to explain any of it — the Court found no exceptional circumstance and dismissed the application. It relied on the Supreme Court&rsquo;s decision in <em>Balmukund Singh Gautam v. State of Madhya Pradesh</em>, (2026) 6 SCC 216, and the Delhi High Court&rsquo;s decision in <em>Vikas Yadav v. State of NCT of Delhi</em>, 2026:DHC:9520, both noted in the order.</p>

<h2>The practical architecture after a proclamation</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Before proclamation:</strong> non-bailable warrants and process under Sections 84 and 85 of the BNSS, 2023 (formerly Sections 82 and 83 CrPC) precede a declaration. Appearing at this stage, or applying to have warrants converted, preserves the full range of bail remedies.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>After proclamation:</strong> anticipatory bail remains maintainable but succeeds only in exceptional cases. The realistic route is often surrender followed by a regular bail application, coupled with an application to recall the proclamation by explaining the absence.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The added exposure:</strong> evading proclamation is itself an offence under Section 209 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 (formerly Section 174A IPC), which compounds the original case with a fresh prosecution.</div>
</div>

<h2>Reading the decision with the case law</h2>
<p>The judgment sits within a settled line of authority treating proclaimed-offender status as a serious, but not absolute, impediment to anticipatory bail. The Supreme Court has repeatedly emphasised that those who defy judicial process cannot ordinarily seek its protection, while leaving a narrow channel for genuinely exceptional cases — for instance, where the proclamation itself was obtained without proper service, or where the underlying FIR discloses no offence against the applicant at all. The burden of proving either lies squarely on the applicant, and it is a heavy one.</p>
<div class="note">Anticipatory bail is now governed by Section 482 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023, the successor to Section 438 CrPC. Nothing in this article is advice on any pending matter; whether a particular applicant can establish exceptional circumstances is intensely fact-specific and should be assessed on the actual record.</div>
